8
00:00:21.359 --> 00:00:27.119
The store-credit wallet as an event-sourced aggregate, and order tracking as CQRS read models.

9
00:00:27.399 --> 00:00:33.564
L1 compares event notification, event-carried state transfer and event sourcing (plain Java).

10
00:00:33.844 --> 00:00:38.281
L2 is a PostgreSQL event store with optimistic concurrency.

11
00:00:38.561 --> 00:00:42.849
A negative control without the version check lets the wallet go overdrawn.

12
00:00:44.199 --> 00:00:47.975
L3 runs projectors tailing the store while writers append.

13
00:00:48.255 --> 00:00:54.805
The naive max position seen checkpoint misses 2,954 of 12,000 events.

14
00:00:55.085 --> 00:01:00.141
A transaction-id-aware checkpoint misses none, and a rebuild repairs the broken model.

15
00:01:00.421 --> 00:01:18.767
L4 loads a 200,437-event aggregate with and without snapshots (180 ms versus 3.4 ms), and upcasts legacy rupee amounts to paise, where a double-based upcaster silently loses 57.40 rupees.

16
00:01:19.047 --> 00:01:25.170
L2, L4 run as real Java (JDBC) against real PostgreSQL 16.

17
