19
00:01:29.683 --> 00:01:30.536
Part 1.

20
00:01:30.816 --> 00:01:32.736
Introduction and Why It Matters.

21
00:01:33.986 --> 00:01:34.903
A story:

22
00:01:35.183 --> 00:01:37.338
Your order is being packed, for three days.

23
00:01:37.618 --> 00:01:40.157
The team rebuilt order tracking with CQRS:

24
00:01:40.437 --> 00:01:44.383
Commands wrote events to an event store table in PostgreSQL.

25
00:01:44.663 --> 00:01:53.133
A projector polled select from events where id:lastid order by id, updated an ordertracking read model, and saved lastid.

26
00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:55.397
The app read only from ordertracking.

27
00:01:55.677 --> 00:01:57.810
It was fast, and load tests passed.

28
00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:05.453
In production, about 1 in 40 orders got stuck in their tracking view: delivered orders still showed Packing.

29
00:02:05.733 --> 00:02:06.907
Nothing had failed.

30
00:02:07.187 --> 00:02:10.323
The events were in the store and the projector had no errors.

31
00:02:10.603 --> 00:02:20.053
The cause took two weeks to find: id was a bigserial, so the id was assigned when the row was inserted, but it became visible when the transaction committed.

32
00:02:20.333 --> 00:02:26.349
Under concurrency, event 1,041 could commit after event 1,042.

33
00:02:26.629 --> 00:02:33.072
The projector read 1,042, saved lastid = 1042, and never looked back.

34
00:02:33.352 --> 00:02:36.125
Event 1,041 was skipped forever.

35
00:02:36.405 --> 00:02:37.877
L3 reproduces it:

36
00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:43.939
2,954 of 12,000 events missed with 8 concurrent writers.

37
00:02:45.289 --> 00:02:48.659
The same quarter, a different team event-sourced the shopping cart.

38
00:02:48.939 --> 00:02:52.267
Every add 1 banana, remove 1 banana was an event.

39
00:02:52.547 --> 00:02:59.929
A year later, carts had up to 40,000 events, loading a cart took seconds, and nobody had ever asked for cart history.

40
00:03:00.209 --> 00:03:07.398
They also needed to change the event format, which meant writing upcasters for four historical versions of ItemAdded.

41
00:03:07.678 --> 00:03:10.025
Both teams used good patterns in the wrong way:

42
00:03:11.375 --> 00:03:16.580
A projector must checkpoint by what is safe to read, not by the highest number it has seen.

43
00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:21.553
In PostgreSQL, use the transaction id and the snapshot's xmin.

44
00:03:21.833 --> 00:03:26.313
Or use a log that assigns positions at commit (Kafka, EventStoreDB).

45
00:03:26.593 --> 00:03:32.972
Event sourcing is for aggregates whose history is the business, like wallets, ledgers, orders and bookings.

46
00:03:33.252 --> 00:03:35.044
It's not for carts or profiles.

47
00:03:35.324 --> 00:03:40.124
Where it is used, it needs snapshots, versioned events and upcasters from day one.

48
00:03:41.474 --> 00:03:42.157
Quote:

49
00:03:42.437 --> 00:03:44.101
The thesis of this chapter.

50
00:03:44.381 --> 00:03:49.671
Events can play three roles: notifications, carriers of state, or the source of truth.

51
00:03:49.951 --> 00:03:57.141
CQRS separates the model you write from the models you read, and those read models are disposable and rebuildable.

52
00:03:57.421 --> 00:04:03.031
Event sourcing makes the log the source of truth, which gives you history, audit and time travel.

53
00:04:03.311 --> 00:04:10.693
In return you must handle concurrency per stream, checkpoints that don't skip events, snapshots, schema versions and rebuilds.

54
00:04:10.973 --> 00:04:13.917
Use each pattern where its benefit is the requirement.

55
00:04:15.267 --> 00:04:17.571
Where this lives in the grocery platform.

56
00:04:17.851 --> 00:04:19.195
Here is a quick table:

57
00:04:19.475 --> 00:04:21.139
Area, and Pattern, and Why.

58
00:04:21.419 --> 00:04:22.912
