107
00:06:29.474 --> 00:06:30.349
Part 2.

108
00:06:30.629 --> 00:06:32.506
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

109
00:06:33.756 --> 00:06:35.569
Intuition: the bank passbook.

110
00:06:35.849 --> 00:06:38.708
An old-style bank passbook doesn't store your balance.

111
00:06:38.988 --> 00:06:44.556
It stores every deposit and withdrawal, and the balance printed on each line is derived from them.

112
00:06:44.836 --> 00:06:46.457
The passbook lines are events.

113
00:06:46.737 --> 00:06:52.753
They're facts in the past tense (Deposited 500 rupeeson 3 March), and they're never erased.

114
00:06:53.033 --> 00:06:57.215
A mistake is corrected with a new line (Reversal of entry 41).

115
00:06:57.495 --> 00:06:59.841
The balance is a projection of those lines.

116
00:07:00.121 --> 00:07:04.068
So is total spent this month and all cheques above 10,000 rupees.

117
00:07:04.348 --> 00:07:06.545
Each is a different view of the same lines.

118
00:07:07.895 --> 00:07:13.229
The branch keeps the passbook, but the head office builds its own reports from copies of the entries.

119
00:07:13.509 --> 00:07:18.906
That's CQRS: writes happen in one place, and reads use models shaped for each question.

120
00:07:19.186 --> 00:07:23.730
After 20 years, the clerk doesn't re-add every line to answer what's the balance?.

121
00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:26.293
They start from last year's closing balance.

122
00:07:26.573 --> 00:07:27.725
That's a snapshot.

123
00:07:28.005 --> 00:07:34.063
When the bank changed from Rs. to ₹ and from annas to paise, old pages weren't rewritten.

124
00:07:34.343 --> 00:07:36.242
Staff learned to read the old format.

125
00:07:36.522 --> 00:07:37.717
That's upcasting.

126
00:07:39.067 --> 00:07:40.837
Three things called event-driven.

127
00:07:41.117 --> 00:07:44.019
Martin Fowler's distinction, which L1 measures:

128
00:07:45.369 --> 00:07:46.713
Here is a quick table:

129
00:07:46.993 --> 00:07:51.899
Style, and Event carries, and Consumer does, and L1 result (order-service down).

130
00:07:52.179 --> 00:07:53.523
Event notification.

131
00:07:53.803 --> 00:07:54.827
Event carries:

132
00:07:55.107 --> 00:07:58.137
The fact + ids (AddressChangedorderId).

133
00:07:58.417 --> 00:07:59.462
Consumer does:

134
00:07:59.742 --> 00:08:01.705
Calls the producer back for details.

135
00:08:01.985 --> 00:08:04.225
L1 result (order-service down):

136
00:08:04.505 --> 00:08:06.638
10,000 callbacks, all failed.

137
00:08:06.918 --> 00:08:09.713
Tight runtime coupling, and load on the producer.

138
00:08:09.993 --> 00:08:12.083
Event-carried state transfer (ECST).

139
00:08:12.363 --> 00:08:13.387
Event carries:

140
00:08:13.667 --> 00:08:17.102
The new state (AddressChangedWithStateorderId, address).

141
00:08:17.382 --> 00:08:18.427
Consumer does:

142
00:08:18.707 --> 00:08:20.030
Keeps a local copy.

143
00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:22.550
L1 result (order-service down):

144
00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:26.009
0 callbacks; the local copy equals the source.

145
00:08:26.289 --> 00:08:27.334
Event sourcing.

146
00:08:27.614 --> 00:08:28.638
Event carries:

147
00:08:28.918 --> 00:08:32.481
Each change as the durable record (AddressSet... version).

148
00:08:32.761 --> 00:08:33.806
Consumer does:

149
00:08:34.086 --> 00:08:35.814
State = fold(events).

150
00:08:36.094 --> 00:08:38.334
L1 result (order-service down):

151
00:08:38.614 --> 00:08:43.798
Same current state, plus the full history (5 versions of ord-985).

152
00:08:45.148 --> 00:08:49.351
Event-driven architecture usually means the first two between services.

153
00:08:49.631 --> 00:08:56.329
Event sourcing is an internal persistence choice of one service or aggregate, and it doesn't require the others.

154
00:08:56.609 --> 00:09:00.300
CQRS is orthogonal to both, and it's common with either.

155
00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:01.689
Choose per event:

156
00:09:01.969 --> 00:09:09.180
Notification when consumers need only the fact, or the data is large or sensitive (fetch with authorization).

157
00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:14.925
ECST when consumers need the data and must keep working when the producer is down.

158
00:09:15.205 --> 00:09:20.261
Never use events as RPC (PleaseSendSms as an event): that's a command.

159
00:09:20.541 --> 00:09:23.143
Send it to a queue with an owner (Ch 14).

160
00:09:24.493 --> 00:09:27.053
Domain events versus integration events.

161
00:09:27.333 --> 00:09:31.195
Domain events live inside a bounded context (WalletDebited).

162
00:09:31.475 --> 00:09:34.077
They can be fine-grained and change with the model.

163
00:09:34.357 --> 00:09:39.499
Integration events are the context's public contract (order.order.placed.v1).

164
00:09:39.779 --> 00:09:49.336
They're stable, versioned and carry what consumers need, and they're usually mapped from domain events in the outbox (Ch 13) or by a translator.

165
00:09:49.616 --> 00:09:54.203
Don't publish internal event-sourcing events directly as integration events.

166
00:09:54.483 --> 00:10:03.784
That couples consumers to your aggregate's internals, and every internal refactor becomes a breaking change (Ch 14 §2.10).

167
00:10:05.134 --> 00:10:06.201
CQRS.

168
00:10:06.481 --> 00:10:11.899
Command Query Responsibility Segregation (Greg Young, building on Meyer's CQS):

169
00:10:12.179 --> 00:10:17.214
The command side validates intentions against an aggregate and records changes.

170
00:10:17.494 --> 00:10:22.785
The query side serves reads from read models (projections) optimized for each query.

171
00:10:24.135 --> 00:10:30.769
On screen: command, then aggregate (write model), then events or changes ─┬, then projector, then ordertracking.

172
00:10:31.049 --> 00:10:34.441
(PostgreSQL). ├, then projector, then storedashboard.

173
00:10:34.721 --> 00:10:38.540
(PostgreSQL, per store). └, then projector, then ordersearch.

174
00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:41.252
(OpenSearch). query ───────────────────────────────────────────────────, then read model.

175
00:10:44.102 --> 00:10:45.446
Here is a quick table:

176
00:10:45.726 --> 00:10:47.027
Benefit, and Cost.

177
00:10:47.307 --> 00:10:51.574
Read models shaped per query (no 7-table joins at read time):

178
00:10:51.854 --> 00:10:54.969
Eventual consistency between write and read (lag).

179
00:10:55.249 --> 00:10:59.387
Read and write scale independently (Ch 11 ordersbystore):

180
00:10:59.667 --> 00:11:02.846
More moving parts: projectors, checkpoints, rebuilds.

181
00:11:03.126 --> 00:11:05.814
Different stores per read (search, cache, graph):

182
00:11:06.094 --> 00:11:09.422
Duplicate data; schema changes touch several models.

183
00:11:09.702 --> 00:11:13.201
Read models are disposable: drop and rebuild (L3:

184
00:11:13.481 --> 00:11:16.745
12,000 events in 549 ms):

185
00:11:17.025 --> 00:11:19.905
Rebuild time grows with history, so plan for it.

186
00:11:21.255 --> 00:11:28.337
CQRS doesn't need event sourcing: the write side can be ordinary tables plus an outbox (Ch 13).

187
00:11:28.617 --> 00:11:30.708
Nor does it need separate databases.

188
00:11:30.988 --> 00:11:34.465
A second table updated by a projector is CQRS.

189
00:11:34.745 --> 00:11:37.028
Read-your-writes on a CQRS system:

190
00:11:37.308 --> 00:11:40.721
Return the new state from the command (the command side knows it).

191
00:11:41.001 --> 00:11:49.108
Or return a version or position, and have the query wait until the read model has reached it (like the Ch 10 LSN token).

192
00:11:49.388 --> 00:11:53.548
Or read from the write model for the user's own freshly changed entity.

193
00:11:54.898 --> 00:11:55.943
Event sourcing.

194
00:11:56.223 --> 00:11:58.186
The aggregate's state isn't stored.

195
00:11:58.466 --> 00:12:01.602
Its events are, and the state is rebuilt by folding them:

196
00:12:02.952 --> 00:12:04.467
Here is the Java code.

197
00:12:07.317 --> 00:12:08.255
What you gain:

198
00:12:08.535 --> 00:12:10.775
A complete audit trail by construction.

199
00:12:11.055 --> 00:12:20.869
L2's running balance at any version: v52, then 50.00, rupeesv101, then 1.00 rupees.

200
00:12:21.149 --> 00:12:24.690
Time travel: what did the wallet look like on 31 March?

201
00:12:24.970 --> 00:12:28.895
New read models from old history (replay into a new projection).

202
00:12:29.175 --> 00:12:31.479
Debugging by replaying a production stream.

203
00:12:31.759 --> 00:12:33.466
Natural integration events.

204
00:12:33.746 --> 00:12:34.642
What you pay:

205
00:12:35.992 --> 00:12:42.819
Every read of current state needs a projection or a replay, so snapshots are needed for long streams (L4).

206
00:12:43.099 --> 00:12:47.173
Event schemas are forever: versioning and upcasting (L4 B).

207
00:12:47.453 --> 00:12:50.035
You need concurrency control per stream (L2).

208
00:12:50.315 --> 00:12:53.579
Projections must be correct under concurrency (L3).

209
00:12:53.859 --> 00:12:58.467
Erasure of personal data in an immutable log (§2.10).

210
00:12:58.747 --> 00:13:03.205
The team must think in events, and queries across aggregates need projections.

211
00:13:04.555 --> 00:13:07.222
The event store and optimistic concurrency.

212
00:13:07.502 --> 00:13:08.846
An event store needs:

213
00:13:09.126 --> 00:13:12.753
Append with an expected version, failing if the stream moved on.

214
00:13:13.033 --> 00:13:14.334
Read a stream in order.

215
00:13:14.614 --> 00:13:21.675
Read all events in a global order for projectors, with a position that is safe to checkpoint (§2.7).

216
00:13:21.955 --> 00:13:30.723
Optionally, subscriptions or notifications, snapshots and metadata (correlation or causation ids, user, HLC timestamp).

217
00:13:32.073 --> 00:13:47.732
In PostgreSQL, primary key (streamid, version) makes the database enforce (1): a concurrent writer that decided on the same version gets a unique violation (23505), reloads and decides again.

218
00:13:48.012 --> 00:13:54.284
L2 ran 16 threads times 20 debits of 1 rupeesagainst a 100 rupeeswallet:

219
00:13:55.634 --> 00:13:56.978
Here is a quick table:

220
00:13:57.258 --> 00:14:01.695
Store, and Debits accepted, and Final balance, and Events sharing a version.

221
00:14:01.975 --> 00:14:03.618
No expected-version check.

222
00:14:03.898 --> 00:14:05.093
Debits accepted:

223
00:14:05.373 --> 00:14:06.653
111.

224
00:14:06.933 --> 00:14:11.498
Final balance: minus 11.00 rupees (overdrawn).

225
00:14:11.778 --> 00:14:13.165
Events sharing a version:

226
00:14:13.445 --> 00:14:14.447
56.

227
00:14:14.727 --> 00:14:17.458
Expected version (PK on stream, version).

228
00:14:17.738 --> 00:14:18.933
Debits accepted:

229
00:14:19.213 --> 00:14:20.151
100.

230
00:14:20.431 --> 00:14:21.583
Final balance:

231
00:14:21.863 --> 00:14:23.997
0.00 rupees.

232
00:14:24.277 --> 00:14:25.663
Events sharing a version:

233
00:14:25.943 --> 00:14:29.229
0, after 649 retried conflicts.

234
00:14:30.579 --> 00:14:39.304
The invariant balance ≥ 0 is enforced only because every decision was made against the exact version it appended after.

235
00:14:39.584 --> 00:14:44.064
The 649 conflicts show the cost of contention on one stream.

236
00:14:44.344 --> 00:14:49.272
A hot stream needs a design fix: smaller aggregates, or commands that commute.

237
00:14:49.552 --> 00:14:51.216
Retrying harder doesn't help.

238
00:14:51.496 --> 00:14:54.803
Stream = aggregate = consistency boundary.

239
00:14:55.083 --> 00:14:57.344
One transaction appends to one stream.

240
00:14:57.624 --> 00:15:08.525
Invariants across streams (for example, a customer's total credit at most 10,000 rupeesacross wallets) need a different aggregate design or a saga (Ch 13).

241
00:15:09.875 --> 00:15:12.094
Projections and the checkpoint problem.

242
00:15:12.374 --> 00:15:13.377
A projector:

243
00:15:13.657 --> 00:15:15.577
Reads events after its checkpoint.

244
00:15:15.857 --> 00:15:17.606
Applies them to a read model.

245
00:15:17.886 --> 00:15:24.563
Stores the new checkpoint in the same transaction as the read-model change, which makes it exactly-once in effect.

246
00:15:24.843 --> 00:15:27.830
Correctness hinges on what after the checkpoint means.

247
00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:37.457
Why max position seen is wrong on a database: sequences hand out numbers at insert, and transactions commit in a different order.

248
00:15:37.737 --> 00:15:46.996
When the projector sees position 1,042 committed and 1,041 still in flight, it advances past 1,041 forever.

249
00:15:47.276 --> 00:15:48.129
L3:

250
00:15:48.409 --> 00:15:57.220
2,954 of 12,000 events missed, and 417 orders show a stale status, with no error anywhere.

251
00:15:57.500 --> 00:15:58.396
Fixes:

252
00:15:59.746 --> 00:16:01.090
Here is a quick table:

253
00:16:01.370 --> 00:16:03.098
Approach, and How, and Trade-off.

254
00:16:03.378 --> 00:16:06.023
Transaction-id bound (PostgreSQL).

255
00:16:06.303 --> 00:16:06.965
How:

256
00:16:07.245 --> 00:16:09.975
Store pgcurrentxactid() with each event.

257
00:16:10.255 --> 00:16:18.682
Read where (txid, position) checkpoint and txid pgsnapshotxmin(pgcurrentsnapshot()), ordered by (txid, position).

258
00:16:18.962 --> 00:16:19.858
Trade-off:

259
00:16:20.138 --> 00:16:24.405
Waits for the oldest open transaction; long transactions add lag.

260
00:16:24.685 --> 00:16:25.538
L3:

261
00:16:25.818 --> 00:16:26.799
0 missed.

262
00:16:27.079 --> 00:16:29.383
Single writer or serialize appends.

263
00:16:29.663 --> 00:16:30.325
How:

264
00:16:30.605 --> 00:16:35.085
Take an advisory lock per append, so commit order = position order.

265
00:16:35.365 --> 00:16:36.261
Trade-off:

266
00:16:36.541 --> 00:16:37.479
Throughput cap.

267
00:16:37.759 --> 00:16:38.869
Commit-ordered log.

268
00:16:39.149 --> 00:16:39.810
How:

269
00:16:40.090 --> 00:16:49.029
Kafka offsets, EventStoreDB or KurrentDB, Axon Server, or CDC from the WAL (commit order, Ch 13).

270
00:16:49.309 --> 00:16:50.205
Trade-off:

271
00:16:50.485 --> 00:16:53.557
Another system, but the log order is the commit order.

272
00:16:53.837 --> 00:16:55.437
Gap detection with a wait.

273
00:16:55.717 --> 00:16:56.378
How:

274
00:16:56.658 --> 00:17:01.159
Treat a missing position as maybe in flight and wait up to T before skipping it.

275
00:17:01.439 --> 00:17:02.335
Trade-off:

276
00:17:02.615 --> 00:17:04.578
Complex; the timeout is a guess.

277
00:17:05.928 --> 00:17:07.464
Idempotent projections:

278
00:17:07.744 --> 00:17:14.891
Projectors are at-least-once (crashes between apply and checkpoint if they aren't in one transaction, or replays).

279
00:17:15.171 --> 00:17:21.357
Use upserts keyed by entity, where lastversion:v guards, or the inbox pattern (Ch 13).

280
00:17:21.637 --> 00:17:27.589
Side effects (emails) don't belong in projectors that get rebuilt, or every rebuild re-sends them.

281
00:17:27.869 --> 00:17:31.944
Put them in separate process managers or reactors with their own dedup.

282
00:17:32.224 --> 00:17:33.077
Rebuilds:

283
00:17:34.427 --> 00:17:36.433
A read model is derived data.

284
00:17:36.713 --> 00:17:38.995
Drop it, reset its checkpoint and replay.

285
00:17:39.275 --> 00:17:43.435
L3 rebuilt the broken model in 549 ms.

286
00:17:43.715 --> 00:17:52.334
For big histories, build the new version alongside the old (ordertrackingv2), then switch reads when it catches up (blue or green projections).

287
00:17:53.684 --> 00:17:54.665
Snapshots.

288
00:17:54.945 --> 00:17:58.039
A snapshot stores an aggregate's state at version N.

289
00:17:58.319 --> 00:18:01.753
Loading = latest snapshot + the events after it.

290
00:18:02.033 --> 00:18:02.908
L4:

291
00:18:03.188 --> 00:18:15.327
200,437 events, full replay 179.8 ms versus snapshot + 437 events 3.4 ms, same state.

292
00:18:15.607 --> 00:18:19.916
Take a snapshot every N events, or when load time exceeds a threshold.

293
00:18:20.196 --> 00:18:23.588
Write it asynchronously; snapshots are caches, not truth.

294
00:18:23.868 --> 00:18:25.553
Version the snapshot format.

295
00:18:25.833 --> 00:18:30.441
On a mismatch, discard it and replay (snapshots are disposable, events aren't).

296
00:18:31.791 --> 00:18:33.946
Long streams are often a modelling smell.

297
00:18:34.226 --> 00:18:44.637
Close accounting periods (book closing: a PeriodClosed event carrying balances, then a new stream per period), or split the aggregate, before reaching for snapshots.

298
00:18:45.987 --> 00:18:47.779
Event versioning and upcasting.

299
00:18:48.059 --> 00:18:50.917
Events are immutable, but understanding improves.

300
00:18:51.197 --> 00:18:53.821
Techniques, from the least to the most invasive:

301
00:18:54.101 --> 00:18:59.520
Additive change: add an optional field with a default, and old events still deserialize.

302
00:18:59.800 --> 00:19:04.003
Weak schema: readers ignore unknown fields and default missing ones.

303
00:19:05.353 --> 00:19:13.118
Upcasting: on read, a chain of pure functions converts v1, then v2, then v3 into the shape the code knows.

304
00:19:13.398 --> 00:19:14.913
Stored events are untouched.

305
00:19:15.193 --> 00:19:17.902
Upcasters must be exact and deterministic:

306
00:19:18.182 --> 00:19:35.057
L4's double-based rupees-to-paise upcaster turned 19.99 into 1,998 paise and was wrong on 5,740 of 100,000 legacy events, 57.40 rupeesin total.

307
00:19:35.337 --> 00:19:37.385
The BigDecimal version was exact.

308
00:19:37.665 --> 00:19:41.782
New event type (WalletDebitedV2), with both handled in apply.

309
00:19:42.062 --> 00:19:50.126
Copy-and-transform migration: rewrite streams into a new store (the last resort, with an audit trail of the migration itself).

310
00:19:51.476 --> 00:20:00.137
Record schemaversion (or the event type version) in every stored event, and test upcasters against real samples from production history.

311
00:20:01.487 --> 00:20:03.685
Personal data in immutable logs.

312
00:20:03.965 --> 00:20:09.895
DPDP (India) and GDPR give people a right to erasure, and logs don't forget.

313
00:20:10.175 --> 00:20:11.050
Options:

314
00:20:11.330 --> 00:20:12.994
Keep PII out of events.

315
00:20:13.274 --> 00:20:17.797
Reference a customer id, and store details in a mutable profile service.

316
00:20:18.077 --> 00:20:23.303
Crypto-shredding: encrypt PII fields with a per-customer key kept in a key store.

317
00:20:23.583 --> 00:20:28.149
To erase, delete the key, and the events stay but the PII is unreadable.

318
00:20:28.429 --> 00:20:31.202
Projections must handle unreadable gracefully.

319
00:20:31.482 --> 00:20:35.962
Tombstones and compaction for Kafka-compacted topics (Ch 14).

320
00:20:36.242 --> 00:20:39.442
Don't forget read models, backups and the data warehouse.

321
00:20:40.792 --> 00:20:46.381
Keep an erasure log of what was erased and when, without the data itself (Ch 36).

322
00:20:47.731 --> 00:20:50.441
Process managers and event-driven workflows.

323
00:20:50.721 --> 00:21:00.747
A process manager (or reactor) reacts to events by issuing commands: when PaymentCaptured and StockReserved both arrived for an order, send ConfirmOrder.

324
00:21:01.027 --> 00:21:09.283
It keeps its own state, keyed by correlation id, and is essentially an orchestration saga driven by events (Ch 13).

325
00:21:09.563 --> 00:21:22.598
Separate reactors that cause side effects from projectors that build read models: projectors must be safe to replay, and reactors must not re-send on replay (dedup by event id, or skip on replay).

326
00:21:23.948 --> 00:21:26.785
When to use ES and CQRS, and when not.

327
00:21:27.065 --> 00:21:28.409
Here is a quick table:

328
00:21:28.689 --> 00:21:31.207
Use event sourcing when..., and Don't, when....

329
00:21:31.487 --> 00:21:35.732
History is the business: ledgers, wallets, bookings, claims, compliance:

330
00:21:36.012 --> 00:21:39.660
Current state is all anyone needs (carts, profiles, settings).

331
00:21:39.940 --> 00:21:43.289
Audit or dispute resolution needs exact who, what, when:

332
00:21:43.569 --> 00:21:47.495
A simple audit table or CDC to a history table would do.

333
00:21:47.775 --> 00:21:51.679
Many future read models are likely (analytics, ML features):

334
00:21:51.959 --> 00:21:54.433
The team is new to it and the domain is CRUD.

335
00:21:54.713 --> 00:21:56.825
Temporal queries (as of) are frequent:

336
00:21:57.105 --> 00:22:02.119
Aggregates would have very long, very hot streams without natural closing periods.

337
00:22:02.399 --> 00:22:05.599
Natural concurrency boundaries (per account or booking):

338
00:22:05.879 --> 00:22:07.969
Cross-aggregate invariants dominate.

339
00:22:09.319 --> 00:22:10.663
Here is a quick table:

340
00:22:10.943 --> 00:22:13.397
Use CQRS when..., and Don't, when....

341
00:22:13.677 --> 00:22:17.026
Read shapes differ a lot from write shapes, or reads ≫ writes:

342
00:22:17.306 --> 00:22:20.122
One table + indexes serves reads fine.

343
00:22:20.402 --> 00:22:23.709
Different read stores are needed (search, cache, analytics):

344
00:22:23.989 --> 00:22:28.341
Users can't tolerate any read lag and the command can't return state.

345
00:22:28.621 --> 00:22:32.354
Read and write scaling differ (Ch 11 ordersbystore):

346
00:22:32.634 --> 00:22:34.277
Small team, small system.

347
00:22:35.627 --> 00:22:40.832
Most of the grocery platform is CRUD + outbox events + a few read models.

348
00:22:41.112 --> 00:22:44.760
Event sourcing is used for the wallet and slot bookings only.

