Command latency grows every month.

870
00:55:49.335 --> 00:55:50.061
Fix:

871
00:55:50.341 --> 00:55:51.706
Snapshots (L4:

872
00:55:51.986 --> 00:55:57.810
180, then 3.4 ms), and period closing for accounting-style streams.

873
00:55:59.160 --> 00:56:00.035
Part 8.

874
00:56:00.315 --> 00:56:01.659
Interview Q and A.

875
00:56:02.909 --> 00:56:03.741
Beginner.

876
00:56:04.021 --> 00:56:05.045
Question 1.

877
00:56:05.325 --> 00:56:07.650
What is event sourcing, in one paragraph?

878
00:56:07.930 --> 00:56:09.295
Thirty-second answer:

879
00:56:09.575 --> 00:56:20.029
Instead of storing an entity's current state and overwriting it, you store every change as an immutable event (Credited 500, rupeesDebited 320 rupees).

880
00:56:20.309 --> 00:56:23.722
The current state is computed by replaying the events in order.

881
00:56:24.002 --> 00:56:29.058
You get a full audit trail, time travel and the ability to build new views from history.

882
00:56:29.338 --> 00:56:33.135
You pay with complexity in versioning, projections and concurrency.

883
00:56:34.485 --> 00:56:35.488
Question 2.

884
00:56:35.768 --> 00:56:37.112
What is CQRS?

885
00:56:37.392 --> 00:56:38.757
Thirty-second answer:

886
00:56:39.037 --> 00:56:52.456
Command Query Responsibility Segregation separates the model that handles writes (commands, validated against an aggregate) from the models that serve reads (read models or projections shaped for each query).

887
00:56:52.736 --> 00:56:57.579
Read models are updated asynchronously from the write side's changes or events.

888
00:56:58.929 --> 00:56:59.974
Question 3.

889
00:57:00.254 --> 00:57:03.667
Event notification versus event-carried state transfer?

890
00:57:03.947 --> 00:57:05.313
Thirty-second answer:

891
00:57:05.593 --> 00:57:10.670
A notification says X changed with an id, and consumers call back for details.

892
00:57:10.950 --> 00:57:16.518
State transfer includes the new data, so consumers keep a local copy and don't call back.

893
00:57:16.798 --> 00:57:21.022
Notifications create runtime coupling and load on the producer.

894
00:57:21.302 --> 00:57:25.163
State transfer creates data duplication and larger events.

895
00:57:26.513 --> 00:57:27.580
Question 4.

896
00:57:27.860 --> 00:57:29.865
What's a projection (read model)?

897
00:57:30.145 --> 00:57:31.511
Thirty-second answer:

898
00:57:31.791 --> 00:57:39.833
A view of data built by processing events, stored in a form that makes a particular query fast (order tracking, a store dashboard).

899
00:57:40.113 --> 00:57:44.657
It's derived data: you can delete it and rebuild it from the events at any time.

900
00:57:46.007 --> 00:57:47.010
Intermediate.

901
00:57:47.290 --> 00:57:48.399
Question 5.

902
00:57:48.679 --> 00:57:52.562
How do you handle concurrent writes to the same aggregate in an event store?

903
00:57:52.842 --> 00:57:54.207
Thirty-second answer:

904
00:57:54.487 --> 00:57:56.109
Optimistic concurrency.

905
00:57:56.389 --> 00:58:01.061
Load the stream (version N), decide, and append with expected version N.

906
00:58:01.341 --> 00:58:04.967
The store rejects the append if the stream is no longer at N.

907
00:58:05.247 --> 00:58:11.178
The caller reloads, re-decides against the new state (which may now reject the command) and retries.

908
00:58:12.528 --> 00:58:13.637
Question 6.

909
00:58:13.917 --> 00:58:17.032
Why can a projector miss events, and how do you prevent it?

910
00:58:17.312 --> 00:58:18.677
Thirty-second answer:

911
00:58:18.957 --> 00:58:25.784
If the global position comes from a database sequence, it's assigned at insert, but the row becomes visible at commit.

912
00:58:26.064 --> 00:58:34.896
Transactions commit out of order, so a projector that checkpoints the highest position seen can move past a position that commits later and never read it.

913
00:58:35.176 --> 00:58:45.352
Prevent it by reading only up to what can't change anymore (PostgreSQL: transaction ids below the snapshot's xmin), or by using a commit-ordered log.

914
00:58:46.702 --> 00:58:47.790
Question 7.

915
00:58:48.070 --> 00:58:50.225
How do you make projections idempotent?

916
00:58:50.505 --> 00:58:51.870
Thirty-second answer:

917
00:58:52.150 --> 00:58:58.742
Store the checkpoint in the same transaction as the read model update, so a crash either applies both or neither.

918
00:58:59.022 --> 00:59:06.915
Make each apply an upsert keyed by entity with a version guard (where lastversion:v), so replays and duplicates are no-ops.

919
00:59:07.195 --> 00:59:09.201
Keep side effects out of projections.

920
00:59:10.551 --> 00:59:11.575
Question 8.

921
00:59:11.855 --> 00:59:13.284
When do you need snapshots?

922
00:59:13.564 --> 00:59:14.929
Thirty-second answer:

923
00:59:15.209 --> 00:59:20.031
When aggregates have long streams, so replaying them on every command costs too much.

924
00:59:20.311 --> 00:59:24.001
Store state every N events and load the snapshot plus the tail.

925
00:59:24.281 --> 00:59:28.719
Snapshots are caches: versioned, disposable, and rebuilt by replay.

926
00:59:30.069 --> 00:59:31.135
Question 9.

927
00:59:31.415 --> 00:59:33.911
Domain events versus integration events?

928
00:59:34.191 --> 00:59:35.557
Thirty-second answer:

929
00:59:35.837 --> 00:59:40.871
Domain events are internal to a bounded context: fine-grained and free to change.

930
00:59:41.151 --> 00:59:47.551
Integration events are the context's public contract: stable, versioned, designed for consumers.

931
00:59:47.831 --> 00:59:53.591
Map domain events to integration events (in the outbox) rather than exposing your event store.

932
00:59:54.941 --> 00:59:55.709
Senior.

933
00:59:55.989 --> 00:59:57.035
Question 10.

934
00:59:57.315 --> 01:00:00.451
How do you evolve event schemas in an event-sourced system?

935
01:00:00.731 --> 01:00:02.096
Thirty-second answer:

936
01:00:02.376 --> 01:00:03.741
Never rewrite history.

937
01:00:04.021 --> 01:00:16.757
Make additive changes where possible, version every event, and convert old versions on read with upcasters (pure, exact, deterministic functions, chained v1, then v2, then v3).

938
01:00:17.037 --> 01:00:23.629
Use a new event type for semantic changes, and copy-and-transform migrations only as a last resort.

939
01:00:24.979 --> 01:00:26.110
Question 11.

940
01:00:26.390 --> 01:00:29.121
How do you rebuild a read model without downtime?

941
01:00:29.401 --> 01:00:30.766
Thirty-second answer:

942
01:00:31.046 --> 01:00:32.710
Blue or green projections.

943
01:00:32.990 --> 01:00:39.369
Build ordertrackingv2 alongside v1 from position 0, in parallel by stream hash if needed.

944
01:00:39.649 --> 01:00:47.371
When v2 has caught up with the live position, switch queries to v2 (a feature flag or view swap), then retire v1.

945
01:00:48.721 --> 01:00:49.809
Question 12.

946
01:00:50.089 --> 01:00:52.927
How do you give users read-your-writes with CQRS?

947
01:00:53.207 --> 01:00:54.572
Thirty-second answer:

948
01:00:54.852 --> 01:01:02.468
The command returns the new version (or global position), and the query waits until the read model has reached it, with a timeout.

949
01:01:02.748 --> 01:01:05.777
Or the command returns the resulting state directly.

950
01:01:06.057 --> 01:01:10.943
Or the UI reads the user's own entity from the write model for a short window.

951
01:01:12.293 --> 01:01:13.573
Question 13.

952
01:01:13.853 --> 01:01:17.821
How does event sourcing interact with sagas and process managers?

953
01:01:18.101 --> 01:01:19.466
Thirty-second answer:

954
01:01:19.746 --> 01:01:21.218
Aggregates emit events.

955
01:01:21.498 --> 01:01:32.826
Process managers subscribe to events, keep their own state per correlation id, and issue commands to other aggregates or services, which is how cross-aggregate workflows run.

956
01:01:33.106 --> 01:01:38.183
They must be idempotent and must not re-run side effects when projections are rebuilt.

957
01:01:39.533 --> 01:01:40.728
Question 14.

958
01:01:41.008 --> 01:01:44.144
What are the hard parts of running event sourcing in production?

959
01:01:44.424 --> 01:01:45.789
Thirty-second answer:

960
01:01:46.069 --> 01:01:58.848
Projection correctness (gaps, idempotency) and lag, rebuild time as history grows, schema evolution forever, hot streams and contention, PII erasure in an immutable log, and team skills.

961
01:01:59.128 --> 01:02:08.749
Each needs tooling: safe checkpoints, reconciliation, blue or green rebuilds, upcaster tests, snapshots or period closing, and crypto-shredding.

962
01:02:10.099 --> 01:02:10.995
Architect.

963
01:02:11.275 --> 01:02:12.470
Question 15.

964
01:02:12.750 --> 01:02:15.331
Should the grocery platform event-source orders?

965
01:02:15.611 --> 01:02:16.977
Thirty-second answer:

966
01:02:17.257 --> 01:02:18.345
Probably not.

967
01:02:18.625 --> 01:02:27.435
The business needs the current order state and a timeline, which tables + outbox events + a CQRS timeline read model provide.

968
01:02:27.715 --> 01:02:33.667
Event-source where history is the product: the wallet ledger (audit, disputes) and slot bookings.

969
01:02:33.947 --> 01:02:38.897
Keep orders in tables with state transitions guarded by versions (Ch 12).

970
01:02:40.247 --> 01:02:41.441
Question 16.

971
01:02:41.721 --> 01:02:46.393
How do you handle GDPR or DPDP erasure with event sourcing?

972
01:02:46.673 --> 01:02:48.039
Thirty-second answer:

973
01:02:48.319 --> 01:02:53.183
Keep personal data out of events (reference ids, details in a mutable store).

974
01:02:53.463 --> 01:02:59.415
Where it must be in events, encrypt it with a per-person key and delete the key to erase (crypto-shredding).

975
01:02:59.695 --> 01:03:06.671
Then propagate erasure to read models, caches, search, backups and the warehouse, and record the erasure itself.

976
01:03:08.021 --> 01:03:09.365
Question 17.

977
01:03:09.645 --> 01:03:14.637
PostgreSQL versus EventStoreDB versus Kafka as the event store?

978
01:03:14.917 --> 01:03:16.282
Thirty-second answer:

979
01:03:16.562 --> 01:03:26.375
PostgreSQL: familiar, transactional with outbox and read models, but you must solve global ordering for projections (xid approach) and scale it yourself.

980
01:03:26.655 --> 01:03:36.085
EventStoreDB or KurrentDB: purpose-built (per-stream expected versions, commit-ordered $all, subscriptions), but another system to run.

981
01:03:36.365 --> 01:03:48.141
Kafka: a great commit-ordered log for integration and replay, but no per-stream expected-version append, and per-entity stream reads are expensive, so it's a poor source of truth for aggregates.

982
01:03:49.491 --> 01:03:50.664
Question 18.

983
01:03:50.944 --> 01:03:54.144
Design the order tracking read model for 10x growth.

984
01:03:54.424 --> 01:03:55.789
Thirty-second answer:

985
01:03:56.069 --> 01:04:05.072
Feed it from Kafka order events, keyed by order, into a tracking store partitioned like the orders (Ch 11) or into a key-value store.

986
01:04:05.352 --> 01:04:10.792
Projectors run in parallel per Kafka partition, with idempotent version-guarded upserts.

987
01:04:11.072 --> 01:04:15.403
Reads by order id and customer id use a secondary read model.

988
01:04:15.683 --> 01:04:21.592
Rebuild via replay from Kafka retention or a compacted snapshot topic, blue or green.

989
01:04:22.942 --> 01:04:24.179
Question 19.

990
01:04:24.459 --> 01:04:27.382
A projection has shown wrong balances for 2 hours.

991
01:04:27.662 --> 01:04:28.665
What do you do?

992
01:04:28.945 --> 01:04:30.310
Thirty-second answer:

993
01:04:30.590 --> 01:04:36.542
Contain it: flag the view as degraded, and serve critical reads from the write model if possible.

994
01:04:36.822 --> 01:04:44.075
Find the cause (a projector bug, a skipped range, an upcaster bug, a poison event) by reconciling against the store.

995
01:04:44.355 --> 01:04:50.009
Fix the code, rebuild blue or green, verify with reconciliation, switch, and postmortem.

996
01:04:50.289 --> 01:04:52.977
Events were never wrong, so no data is lost.

997
01:04:54.327 --> 01:04:55.436
Question 20.

998
01:04:55.716 --> 01:04:58.959
What would make you reject event sourcing in a design review?

999
01:04:59.239 --> 01:05:00.604
Thirty-second answer:

1000
01:05:00.884 --> 01:05:06.153
No requirement that needs history (audit, temporal queries, many future read models).

1001
01:05:06.433 --> 01:05:09.015
Mostly CRUD with cross-aggregate invariants.

1002
01:05:09.295 --> 01:05:16.676
A team without ES experience and no time to build tooling (projections, upcasting, rebuilds, reconciliation).

1003
01:05:16.956 --> 01:05:19.729
Heavy PII without an erasure strategy.

1004
01:05:20.009 --> 01:05:25.001
Or aggregates that would become very long or hot streams without natural boundaries.

1005
01:05:26.351 --> 01:05:27.247
Part 9.

1006
01:05:27.527 --> 01:05:28.935
Summary and Practice.

1007
01:05:30.185 --> 01:05:31.273
Key takeaways.

1008
01:05:31.553 --> 01:05:38.892
Events play three roles: notification (with coupling), state transfer (with duplication), and source of truth (with history).

1009
01:05:39.172 --> 01:05:40.708
L1 measured the difference.

1010
01:05:40.988 --> 01:05:44.231
CQRS separates writes from purpose-built reads.

1011
01:05:44.511 --> 01:05:51.209
Read models are derived, disposable and rebuildable (549 ms for 12 K events).

1012
01:05:51.489 --> 01:05:54.113
Event sourcing: state = fold(events).

1013
01:05:54.393 --> 01:05:59.151
You get audit and time travel, and pay in versioning, projections and concurrency.

1014
01:05:59.431 --> 01:06:07.473
Expected-version appends protect invariants (L2: overdraft minus 11 rupeeswithout them, 0 rupeeswith them).

1015
01:06:08.823 --> 01:06:19.213
Projection checkpoints must be safe under concurrent commits: naive positions missed 2,954 of 12,000 events, and xid-safe ones missed 0.

1016
01:06:19.493 --> 01:06:27.855
Projections are idempotent: the checkpoint is updated in the same transaction, upserts are version-guarded, and there are no side effects.

1017
01:06:28.135 --> 01:06:35.389
Snapshots and period closing bound load time (179.8 ms, then 3.4 ms).

1018
01:06:35.669 --> 01:06:36.970
Never rewrite events.

1019
01:06:37.250 --> 01:06:43.565
Version them and upcast exactly (a double upcaster lost 57.40 rupees).

1020
01:06:43.845 --> 01:06:46.554
Keep PII out of events, or crypto-shred it.

1021
01:06:47.904 --> 01:06:53.664
Use event sourcing where history is the business, and CRUD + outbox + read models elsewhere.

